course: biology
lesson: 25
title: Horses, zebras and donkeys
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A horse is a runner built to escape: four long legs, each ending in a single giant toe whose hoof is a huge toenail, eyes on the sides of its head that see almost all the way round, and a big heart and lungs for galloping.
explain: It can even doze standing up, because its legs lock with a system called the stay apparatus, so it is ready to run the moment a predator appears.
explain: Its cousins are the zebra, black-skinned with white stripes that seem to confuse biting flies, and the donkey, a desert animal with long ears and a hee-haw bray that carries about 3 kilometres.
explain: Old paintings and cartoons show a galloping horse with its front legs stretched forwards and back legs stretched back like a rocking horse; Eadweard Muybridge's photographs in 1878 showed that all four feet do leave the ground, but with the legs tucked under the body.
explain: And Eeyore is gloomy and cartoon donkeys are stubborn and stupid, but real donkeys are careful: they stop when they judge something unsafe.
fact: Horses can sleep standing up because a locking system in their legs, the stay apparatus, holds them up, though they still lie down for short times for deep sleep.
fact: A hoof is made of keratin like your fingernails, so a farrier's nails in a properly fitted shoe don't hurt; a horse walks on the tip of one toe on each leg.
fact: Horses' eyes are on the sides of the head and see almost all round, with small blind spots right behind and right in front of the nose, so a horse startled from behind may kick.
fact: Horse teeth keep growing out and wearing down on tough grass, so a vet can guess a horse's age from its teeth.
fact: A foal can usually stand within about an hour of birth and run within hours, and its legs are nearly as long as an adult's.
fact: A galloping racehorse can reach about 70 kilometres an hour over a short distance.
fact: Every zebra has its own stripe pattern, and the best evidence says the stripes help keep biting flies from landing.
fact: Zebras have black skin, so they are usually described as black with white stripes.
fact: Zebras defend themselves with powerful kicks and bites that can badly injure a lion, and they have never been tamed like horses.
fact: Donkeys bray to call other donkeys, and the sound can carry about 3 kilometres across their desert homeland.
fact: Donkeys' long ears hear far-away calls and help them lose heat, and donkeys are often kept as guards for sheep, because they chase off foxes and dogs.
fact: A mule is the young of a donkey father and a horse mother; it is strong and sure-footed but almost never has young of its own.
fact: Horses roll to scratch itches and coat themselves in dust, but a horse that rolls a lot and seems in pain may have colic, so owners call a vet.
exercise: How can a horse sleep standing up? | Its legs lock with a system called the stay apparatus | It leans its whole weight against the fence or a tree every time | It never really sleeps at all | It balances on its tail like a tripod
exercise: Why is it useful for a horse to doze on its feet? | It can run away from a predator at once | It keeps its hooves warm | It helps it digest grass | It stops the farrier and the vet from getting near its legs
exercise: What is a hoof made of? | Keratin, like your fingernails | Bone, like your skull | Cartilage, like your ears | Wood-like fibre, like the bark of a tree
exercise: Why doesn't nailing on a horseshoe hurt when it is done properly? | The nails go only into the hoof wall, which has no feeling | Horses can't feel anything in their legs | The farrier always puts the horse fast asleep with a needle first | The nails are made of soft rubber
exercise: Why are a horse's eyes on the sides of its head? | So it can see danger coming from almost any direction | So it can read things close up | So it can see in total darkness | So it can look up at the sky and the birds while it is grazing
exercise: Where are a horse's blind spots? | Right behind it and right in front of its nose | Only on its left side | Straight above it in the sky | Nowhere at all, it sees in every direction at once
exercise: How can a vet guess a horse's age? | By looking at its teeth | By counting its hooves and legs | By measuring its tail | By weighing its mane
exercise: How soon can a newborn foal usually stand? | Within about an hour | After about a month | After about a year | Only after it has learned to walk on a lead
exercise: About how fast can a racehorse gallop over a short distance? | About 70 km an hour | About 20 km an hour | About 200 km an hour | About 7 km an hour
exercise: What do scientists think zebra stripes are mainly for? | Keeping biting flies from landing | Scaring lions with bright colours | Helping zebras stay warm at night | Showing which zebra is the oldest
exercise: What colour is a zebra's skin under its fur? | Black | White | Striped | Pink
exercise: How does a zebra fight back against a lion? | With powerful kicks and bites | By changing its stripes to hide | By roaring back louder | By climbing a tree
exercise: Why do donkeys bray so loudly? | To keep in touch with other donkeys spread far apart | Because they are always angry | Because they are hungry every hour | To scare off the dark rain clouds over the fields
exercise: Why do donkeys have such long ears? | To hear far-away calls and to lose heat | To swat flies off their backs | To help them balance on narrow mountain paths | To store water for the desert
exercise: What is a mule? | The young of a donkey father and a horse mother | A small kind of wild horse | The young of a zebra father and a donkey mother | A horse that has lost its mane
exercise: Why might a horse roll on its back in a dusty paddock? | To scratch itches and coat itself in dust | Because it has forgotten how to stand up properly | To flatten the grass for sleeping | To show it wants to be ridden
test: in the old painting the horse gallops with legs stretched out front and back like a rocking horse. is that right? | No, when all four feet are off the ground the legs are tucked under | Yes, that is exactly how horses gallop | No, a galloping horse always keeps two feet down | Yes, but only racehorses do it
test: Count them: a horse walks on how many toes altogether? | 4, one on each leg | 20, five on each leg | 16, four on each leg | 8, two on each leg
test: Why should you never walk straight up behind a horse? | It can't see you there and may kick in fright | Horses only like being stroked on the tail, never on the face | The back of a horse is the hottest part | Horses are deaf from behind
test: My cousin says zebras are white with black stripes. What do most scientists say? | They are black-skinned, so black with white stripes | She is right, the skin under the hair is white | They are grey with no real stripes | It changes every year
test: Shrek's donkey never shuts up and cartoon donkeys are thick. What's true about real donkeys? | They are careful and stop when something seems unsafe | They are the least clever of all the animals on a farm | They follow anyone anywhere | They can't learn their own names
test: shut it with the hee-haw ya daft donkey!! how far can that racket carry? | About 3 kilometres | About 30 metres | About 300 kilometres | Only across one field
test: Why are mules great for carrying bags up a steep mountain path? | They mix a horse's strength with a donkey's sure feet | They can climb using their hands | They never get tired at all | They are lighter than goats
test: What would happen if a horse's teeth stopped growing but it kept eating tough grass all its life? | They would wear down until it could hardly chew | They would get sharper every year | Nothing, teeth never wear down | It would grow a whole new set of teeth every spring
test: A horse keeps rolling, sweating and looking at its belly. What should the owner do? | Call a vet, it may be colic | Leave it, rolling is always for fun | Give it a big meal straight away | Gallop it hard to shake it off
test: Why do people keep a donkey in a field of sheep? | Donkeys chase off foxes and dogs | Donkeys eat the sheep's ticks | Donkeys keep the sheep warm at night | Sheep teach donkeys to be calm
test: Why do you think nobody rides zebras in a race, even though they look like horses? | Zebras have never been tamed; they are too nervous and aggressive | Zebras are too slow to run | The stripes make riders dizzy | Zebras can't carry anything at all, as their backs are far too weak
exam: Identify the anatomical arrangement which permits the horse to remain upright while dozing. | The stay apparatus of the limbs | The hollow structure of the hooves | The lateral position of the eyes | The continual growth of the teeth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the parentage of a mule? | It is the offspring of a male donkey and a female horse | It is the offspring of a male horse and a female zebra | It is the offspring of two wild horses | It is the offspring of a male zebra and a female donkey
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the function of the zebra's stripes. | They appear chiefly to deter biting flies from alighting | They serve principally to frighten lions | They serve principally to retain heat during the cold of the night | They indicate the age of each individual
exam: It is given that the photographs of Eadweard Muybridge were published in 1878. Ascertain what they first demonstrated. | That a galloping horse has all four feet off the ground at one moment | That horses sleep while standing | That a horse sees almost all the way round | That zebras cannot be tamed
